00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the race to find alternatives to russian  energy has accelerated after moscow's  decision to cut its supply of natural  gas to bulgaria and poland is another  very stark reminder that we need to work  with reliable partners and build our  energy independence  the european union gets around 40  percent of its gas from russia  but as the war in ukraine rages on the  block said it aims to slash two-thirds  of its purchases by the end of the year  to help cover this shortfall the u.s has  ramped up shipments of liquefied natural  gas or lng to its western allies but  this move has also begun to drain  domestic supplies and is pushing up  prices in the u.s  we're pretty much tapped out um there's  there's nothing more  to send  here's why providing natural gas from  the u.s is so complicated and what  energy producers are trying to do about  it  even before russia invaded ukraine  domestic inventories of natural gas  across the us were already lean due to  various factors  there have been extreme weather events  like a cold snap in texas that paralyzed  power plants and helped drain global  stockpiles  plus during the pandemic u.s producers  tapped into their inventory of already  drilled wells amassed over years instead  of drilling new costly wells draining  their inventory kept them afloat  financially but has limited their  ability to grow production now that  fossil fuel demand is growing  then came russia's invasion of ukraine  and heightened calls on the eu to reduce  its reliance on russian energy and to  help with that transition president  biden made a pledge we're taking  additional steps in lockstep with our  allies and partners to raise the  economic pressure on putin these steps  come at a time when the amount of gas in  storage in the lower 48 states is 17  below the five-year average for this  time of year  despite dwindling supply the u.s now  aims to send 50 billion cubic meters of  lng to europe every year until at least  2030. that would make up for about a  third of the gas the eu receives from  russia  since the war that the u.s has become  the largest supplier of liquefied  natural gas to europe but there's a  limit to how much the u.s can supply  and for american producers looking to  supply more lng overseas it's no easy  feat that's because transporting natural  gas is more complicated than oil or coal  after the gas is drilled from the ground  it's put through a pipeline it's then  sent to a plant that functions like a  massive refrigerator where it's chilled  into a liquid state for shipping  that super refrigerator that turns it  into a liquid those are multi-billion  dollar facilities the ones that are  running that we have built right now are  running at full capacity and building  these super refrigerators could take  years really the earliest that  additional  u.s supplies could be put on the market  to europe or anywhere  is  20 25 2026. the federal energy  regulatory commission has given the  green light to build 13 new lng  facilities in the u.s but companies  haven't started construction largely  because many haven't been able to secure  the financing and some producers are  worried about whether there'll be a  demand for lng in the long run amid a  global transition to cleaner energy  what they need is  commercial certainty that  there's demand for this product and and  not just demand now but demand over the  long term so usually these projects are  seeking  20-year supply deals because it can take  as much as seven years to even begin  seeing a return  but in the short term as these options  are debated consumers will feel the  pinch we are now the largest exporter of  natural gas in the world so  there's just more demand  for the gas coming out of the u.s and  that's going to push prices higher for  the foreseeable future

